</div>

<div class="md">
## Paul Otlet and the Mundaneum: the library that wanted to be the world

The final layer is the one that was missing from the story for a hundred years, because it was almost lost. In 1910 two Belgian lawyers — \cite[Paul Otlet]{paulotlet} and Henri La Fontaine — built the **Mundaneum** in Brussels \cite[the Mundaneum, Wikipedia]{mundaneum}. It was a *documentation of the world*: every book, article, and fact they could collect reduced to a standardized index card, classified by the Universal Decimal Classification and **cross-indexed**, so that a single fact sat in many drawers at once. At its height the building held more than twelve million cards — a physical model of the whole of human knowledge, *networked by index* \cite[the Mundaneum, Wikipedia]{mundaneum}.

\marginfig{paul_otlet.jpg}{\cite[Paul Otlet (1868–1944)]{paulotlet} at his office, 1937. Public domain (Mundaneum).}
</div>

<div class="md">
### 1934: the paper internet

In 1934 Otlet published the \cite[Traité de Documentation]{traitededocumentation}, and with it the vision that the next forty years would chase. He described a network of what he called "electric telescopes" — a *worldwide network* (a *réseau mondial*) of interlinked documents, held in a central repository, that anyone could consult from their own armchair: a moving image of the world, projected on a personal screen \cite[Wright, Cataloging the World (2014)]{catalogingtheworld}. He called that central repository the **mundotheque** — his word for a "world-library" that would display any document on demand, from anywhere \cite[the Mundaneum, Wikipedia]{mundaneum}.

Read that again: a network of linked documents, *retrievable by query, from a distance, on demand*. In 1934, with paper cards and telegraph lines, Otlet was describing a **paper internet** — before the word "internet" was ever applied to a network of computers. His 1934 vision directly prefigured the *Memex* that Vannevar Bush would make famous a decade later, and the [World Wide Web](untold_history.php) that came after that. This is the hinge the course has been building toward: the moment the *corpus* stops being a thing you read in a room and becomes a thing you **query from anywhere**.

\marginfig{palais_mondial_1930.jpg}{Otlet and Henri La Fontaine at the gates of the Palais Mondial, 1930 — the physical home of the Mundaneum's twelve million cross-indexed cards. Public domain (Mundaneum).}
</div>

<div class="md">
## The payoff: four layers, one system

Step back, and this whole chapter is the slow construction of a **system for information**, in four layers — each a displaced prerequisite of the one you use today:

* **A market** — news as a priced, shipped commodity (the Fugger network, 16th c.).
* **A public** — a venue where the commodity is shared and argued (the coffee house, 17th–18th c.).
* **A corpus** — the attempt to collect and cross-link all of it (the Encyclopédie, 1751–72).
* **A network** — the attempt to make the whole of it retrievable on demand (Otlet's paper internet, 1934).

The mapping onto the present is not decoration. A search engine is the coffee house made global. A [vector database](vectorsearch.php) is the Mundaneum's twelve million cards, now floating in a metric space and retrieved by similarity instead of by drawer. And the [training corpus](training_data.php) — the entire crawlable web that modern models are trained on — is the Encyclopédie's dream of *all knowledge, cross-referenced*, finally achieved at a scale Diderot could not have imagined. The next chapter, [The Untold History of AI](untold_history.php), follows this arc to its end: Vannevar Bush, whose *Memex* of 1945 carried Otlet's "read the world from your armchair" vision into the age of electronics, and the chain that became the web.

> Information became a **commodity** (the Fugger news market), then a **public** (the coffee house), then a **corpus** (the Encyclopédie), and finally a **network** — Otlet's Mundaneum and 1934 "paper internet," a Memex before Bush. Every layer is a displaced prerequisite of the retrieval system you use today.
</div>
